From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:17:03 +0200
Subject: Infer a closure argument's own captures on its inline proxy, within
 its parameter's bound

Since upstream #26989 an inline method's parameter proxies have explicit
types, which capture checking takes at face value, and the argument is checked
only against its proxy. A proxy for a closure argument is typed with the
closure's type from Typer, which is precise about parameters and result but
says nothing about what the closure captures, so any closure that captured a
capability was rejected, even for a `=>` or `?=>` parameter.

The closure's own capture set now follows its parameter's function type:
inferred where the parameter may capture anything (rather than a fresh `any`,
which separation checking sees as hiding what the closure captures), the
declared set where there is one, empty for a pure function. The same applies
to a function in the closure's result; parameter and result types stay
declared, so upstream's cc-inline1 still catches every leak.

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude Code).

Proscala-Patch: proxycap
